  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 341 PM CDT Mon Jul 20 2026\n\n...Synopsis...\n\nUpper ridge that has been driving our region's weather for the\npast week or two has flattened some with the approach of a\nseasonably strong upper shortwave trough moving through ON into\nthe northern Great Lakes region. This is allowing cooler,\nrelatively drier air into the region from the north behind a\nseries of cold fronts. \n\nBehind these fronts today and tonight will allow temperatures \nto be closer to average, as well as bringing gusty northwest / \nnorth winds today, tonight, and Tuesday. Relatively cooler and \ndrier air aloft atop of seasonable low level moisture combined \nwith forcing aloft from the passing shortwave trough will allow \nwidely scattered showers and storms to encroach upon our area\nfrom north to south, generally after 5 PM into this evening.\nIncreased momentum in the lower to mid level portions of the\ncloud-bearing layer will allow gusty downdrafts resultant from\nstorms - some of which may be in the 40 to 50 mph range.\n\nWinds will stay gusty overnight behind a reinforcing cold front\ncoincident with moderately strengthen surface pressure rises.\nWinds reinvigorate out of the north Tuesday afternoon with the\nhelp of daytime mixing and increased flow aloft lingering within\nthe region.\n\nDaytime RH values starting tomorrow and lasting into mid to \nlate week are forecast in the 30-50% range. This combined with\ngusty winds tomorrow, and potentially more gusty winds late in\nthe week combined with warming temperatures, will drive\npotential to see fire weather concerns. This is also driven by\nrapidly drying fuels within the region after recent spell of hot\nand dry conditions.",
